"Encodes many aligned time series as colored cells, retaining temporal patterns while avoiding crossing paths.\n\nUse when: Dozens of series share a common time grid. Pattern comparison matters more than exact point-by-point numeric reading.\n\nAvoid when: Precise small differences cannot be reliably distinguished through color.\n\nVisual grammar: Columns encode ordered time intervals and rows identify series. Cell color encodes the measured value or a clearly named normalized deviation.\n\nBuild: Aggregate observations to one value per series and time cell. Choose a meaningful row order and a fixed temporal grid. Apply a shared color scale and a separate style for missing values.\n\nCheck: Check timestamps align across every row. Verify normalization is labeled and is consistent with the intended comparison. Confirm missing cells cannot be mistaken for zero or neutral deviation.\n\nPitfalls: Independent row normalization conceals absolute differences and must be disclosed. Avoid arbitrary row orders that imply clusters unsupported by the data.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic hourly temperatures for forty sensors, one sensor per row and one color scale across the grid.\n\nAlternatives: Line chart, Horizon chart, Clustered heatmap."
